On Fri, Feb 4, 2011 at 9:02 PM, Luke Bergen <lukeabergen@gmail.com> wrote:

>
> It seems like the writers of the CLL originally did really just think of
> lujvo as being shortened forms of tanru who have been given
> an explicit meaning instead of the vague meaning that tanru have.
>
>
While I fully agree and support that lujvo do not need to be based on tanru,
still I would suggest not to walk too much on the wild side.

I would still prefer to start with a tanru (if possible) and then move
toward more complex sentence as a base for a new lujvo.

Sure, I can make a lujvo and give it whatever meaning and place structure I
please but how good would it be?

I think you can tell that a lujvo is a good one if you are able to guess the
meaning.

{rolcatra} seems rather good to me : {ro} + {catra}  -> "all" "killer" ->
"killer of all" (omincide) -> {ko'a catra ro ko'e ko'i}

I should really do an effort to think something differnt.

remod.

